From 49206428619aff2b784394e66cf0b6f5711f37fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 06:28:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/20] feat(reconciler): redeploy a service whose instance is
 lost

A Ready service whose instance did not survive a restart turned
Degraded and stayed there until it was deleted, and an instance lost
between a successful run and the Ready write failed the service. Both
now redeploy the same generation on the allocation the service holds.

check_instances replaces mark_lost_instances: it still turns Ready
services with a lost instance Degraded (CAS on the version it read)
and also reports Degraded services whose instance runs again. A
Degraded service whose latest run succeeded and whose instance runs
goes back to Ready; otherwise the reconciler stops the previous
deployment and starts the next attempt of the generation's run
(deployment:{uid}:{generation}:{attempt}). The policy is not evaluated
again: the generation was authorised when its deployment started.
Verifying and Updating redeploy the same way when the instance of a
successful run is gone; a failed redeployment there still fails the
service.

Redeployments back off per resource, in memory: the first starts at
once, the next ones at least 10 s after the previous start, doubling
up to 300 s, starting over after 600 s, for a new generation and
after a restart. While it waits after a failed redeployment the Ready
condition (WorkflowFailed) says why.

The manager gives Degraded its own dedup operation ("recover"), so
the key recorded when the generation became Ready does not
short-circuit recovery, and returning to Ready records no key. A
round that read the service as Ready can no longer overwrite a
concurrent InstanceLost report either. The compose smoke now expects
the restarted service back Ready on a new instance.
